While the MacBook Neo achieves a breakthrough &#36;599 starting price, that of course comes with <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/03/04/macbook-neo-compromises/">some compromises</a>, and one of them is slower SSD speeds.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/03/macbook-neo-new-lime.jpg" alt="" width="2500" height="1406" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1039408" /><br/>
<a href="https://www.theverge.com/tech/891741/apple-macbook-neo-a18-pro-review"><em>The Verge</em> today showed</a> the MacBook Neo had up to 8× slower sustained SSD read and write speeds in a benchmark test compared to the new MacBook Pro models with M5 Pro and M5 Max chips. The site did not mention which tool it used to measure SSD speeds, but it was likely Blackmagic's Disk Speed Test or AmorphousDiskMark.<br/>
<br/>
Here is a comparison of sustained SSD speeds, according to <em>The Verge</em>.<br/>
<br/>
